"Translates prioritized customer needs into technical or service characteristics while making their relationships and conflicts visible.\n\nUse when: Connect design targets to the voice of the customer. Discuss competing technical changes before setting specifications.\n\nAvoid when: Customer needs have not been researched or the relationship scores would be unsupported.\n\nVisual grammar: Need rows intersect characteristic columns in a relationship matrix. A roof matrix shows correlations among characteristics. Priority, benchmarking and target areas retain their distinct units and meaning.\n\nBuild: Gather and prioritize customer needs in their own terms. Identify measurable characteristics that can influence those needs. Assess relationships and technical conflicts before agreeing target values.\n\nCheck: Needs are distinct from predetermined technical solutions. Relationship strengths use a defined evidence-based convention. Targets specify direction, units and a credible assessment method.\n\nPitfalls: Filling every intersection with a strong relationship. Using weighted totals to hide major technical tradeoffs.\n\nExample: A reusable bottle team connects easy carrying, cleaning and leak prevention to weight, opening size and seal performance targets.\n\nAlternatives: Kano model, Morphological chart."
